winex11: show an arrow for a cursor set from another process.

WebView2's browser process (msedgewebview2.exe) calls SetCursor for the
window its host process owns; Wine forwards the handle to the window's
process, which cannot resolve an icon object that lives in another process
(win32u: "icon handle ... from other process"), so create_cursor fails. The
driver then returned without touching the window, leaving it on the cursor
set when the pointer entered: the empty one, as the host's own thread has no
cursor. The pointer vanished over every WebView2 window (Minimal Hub; Wine
bug 58922, the same diagnosis in its last comment). Define the theme's arrow
instead. The real shape (hand, I-beam) is not known on this side.

--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
@@ -213,7 +213,19 @@ static void set_window_cursor( Window window, HCURSOR handle )
     else if (XFindContext( gdi_display, (XID)handle, cursor_context, (char **)&cursor ))
     {
         /* try to create it */
-        if (!(cursor = create_cursor( handle ))) return;
+        if (!(cursor = create_cursor( handle )))
+        {
+            /* A handle from another process (WebView2's browser sets the cursor of
+             * its host's window) cannot be resolved here; leaving the window alone
+             * keeps it on the empty cursor set when the pointer entered. Show the
+             * theme's arrow instead. */
+            WARN( "cannot create cursor %p (from another process?), using the default arrow\n", handle );
+#ifdef SONAME_LIBXCURSOR
+            if (pXcursorLibraryLoadCursor) cursor = pXcursorLibraryLoadCursor( gdi_display, "left_ptr" );
+#endif
+            if (!cursor) cursor = XCreateFontCursor( gdi_display, XC_left_ptr );
+            if (!cursor) return;
+        }

         XLockDisplay( gdi_display );
         if (!XFindContext( gdi_display, (XID)handle, cursor_context, (char **)&prev ))
